From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sinful1992 <sinful1992@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 19:27:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Log where a video stream request spends its time before the
 first byte

Some direct-play starts on the Living Room TV hold the first full-file
GET for ~9.3 s (normally 0.13 s) while PlaybackInfo stays at ~60 ms and
the server log says nothing. A manual repro on 2026-09-28 showed the
hold is before the first byte, i.e. server side, but not where.

Every /Videos/{id}/stream request now logs one line when its response
starts: total time to first byte, status, client address, Range, and
the time spent in each phase -- ticket redemption, model binding, item
lookup, static media sources, symlink resolution, each dynamic media
source provider (timed individually; they run in parallel), media
source attach, result, and first byte (file open + first read, inside
PhysicalFileResult). Under 1 s it is Information; 1 s or more is
Warning. Other ticketed routes (audio, HLS segments) log only when
slow, to keep segment traffic out of the log. A request that never
sends a byte logs at completion instead.

The timer lives in HttpContext.Items and is made current through an
AsyncLocal at the top of the action, so MediaSourceManager can mark its
phases without a reference to the request; Mark/Note with no active
timer are no-ops, so scans and PlaybackInfo pay nothing. It cannot be
made current from the auth handler: a value set inside an awaited call
does not flow back out (covered by a test).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
